course: geography
lesson: 39
title: Odd places: microstates, enclaves and disputed lands
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Most countries are big blobs on the map, but a few are so tiny you could walk across them before lunch: Vatican City is smaller than many city parks, and Monaco is the second smallest country in the world.
explain: In Europa Universalis 5 the Papal States cover a big part of central Italy, and dozens of little Italian states sit beside them; almost all were swallowed up by Italy by 1870, but San Marino and the Vatican survive.
explain: Some places are islands of one country inside another, like Lesotho inside South Africa, and in the town of Baarle the Belgian–Dutch border runs through streets and even through houses.
explain: Other places are argued over, like a toy two children both claim: Gibraltar, the Falklands, Kashmir and Taiwan are each claimed by more than one side.
explain: And one whole continent belongs to no country at all: under the Antarctic Treaty of 1959, Antarctica is kept for peace and science.
fact: Vatican City is the smallest country in the world, at about 0.44 square kilometres, and Monaco is the second smallest.
fact: In EU5 the Papal States cover a large part of central Italy, and they were lost to Italy by 1870, leaving only the tiny Vatican City later.
fact: San Marino, inside Italy, claims to be the oldest surviving republic in the world, traditionally founded in 301; it sits on a mountain, stayed small and neutral, and was mostly left alone when Italy was united.
fact: Liechtenstein lies between Switzerland and Austria, and Andorra lies in the Pyrenees between France and Spain.
fact: Andorra has two co-princes, the Bishop of Urgell in Spain and the President of France.
fact: An enclave is a piece of one country completely surrounded by another, such as Lesotho inside South Africa, or San Marino and Vatican City inside Italy.
fact: Kaliningrad is part of Russia but separated from the rest of Russia, lying between Poland and Lithuania; as Königsberg it was once the capital of the dukes of Prussia.
fact: In the town of Baarle, the border between Belgium and the Netherlands weaves through streets and even houses, making dozens of tiny enclaves.
fact: Gibraltar, at the southern tip of Spain, guards the narrow strait between the Atlantic and the Mediterranean; it has been a British territory since 1713, and Spain still claims it.
fact: The Falkland Islands in the South Atlantic are a British territory that Argentina claims as the Malvinas, and the two countries fought a short war over them in 1982.
fact: Kashmir is divided between India, Pakistan and China, and India and Pakistan have fought wars over it.
fact: No country owns Antarctica, and under the Antarctic Treaty of 1959 it is kept for peace and science; about a dozen countries' claims are frozen by the treaty.
fact: Taiwan has its own government, army and elections, but China claims it, and most countries do not officially recognise it as a separate state.
exercise: What is the smallest country in the world? | Vatican City | Monaco | San Marino | Liechtenstein
exercise: Which is the second smallest country in the world? | Monaco | Andorra | Luxembourg | Malta
exercise: About how big is Vatican City? | About 0.44 square kilometres | About 44 square kilometres | About 440 square kilometres | About 4,400 square kilometres
exercise: Which country claims to be the oldest surviving republic in the world? | San Marino | The Netherlands | Switzerland | Liechtenstein
exercise: When was San Marino traditionally founded? | 301 | 1337 | 1492 | 1945
exercise: Where is Liechtenstein? | Between Switzerland and Austria | Between France and Spain in the Pyrenees | Inside Italy, near Rome | On an island in the Baltic
exercise: Who are the heads of state of Andorra? | The Bishop of Urgell and the President of France | The King of Spain and the Pope in Rome, who share the job between them | The President of Italy alone | An elected Doge chosen for life by the nobles
exercise: What is an enclave? | A piece of one country completely surrounded by another | A country with no coast at all | An island far out at sea, a long way from any other land | A capital city of a small state
exercise: Which African country is completely surrounded by South Africa? | Lesotho | Botswana | Mozambique | Zimbabwe
exercise: Why is Kaliningrad cut off from the rest of Russia? | It lies between Poland and Lithuania, away from the rest of Russia | It is on an island far out in the Pacific Ocean, near the north of Japan | A wall was built round it | It sank below the sea
exercise: How can a house in Baarle be in two countries? | The Belgian–Dutch border runs right through it | It has been moved across the border on big wheels, and back again | It floats on a river border | Two families argue over it
exercise: Since when has Gibraltar been British? | 1713 | 1492 | 1945 | 1982
exercise: What does Argentina call the Falkland Islands? | The Malvinas | The Canary Islands | The Azores | The Galápagos
exercise: Which countries share Kashmir? | India, Pakistan and China | India, Nepal and Bhutan | Pakistan, Iran and Afghanistan | China, Russia and Mongolia
exercise: Who does Antarctica belong to? | No country; it is kept for peace and science | The United Kingdom, ever since Captain Scott reached the Pole | Australia | The United Nations owns it as a country
exercise: Why do only a few countries officially recognise Taiwan? | China claims it, and most countries avoid recognising it | Taiwan has no government of its own and is run by nobody | Taiwan is part of Japan | Taiwan has never held elections
test: lol Vatican City is a city not a country. right? | Wrong, it's the smallest country in the world | Right, it's just part of Rome | Right, it belongs to Italy | Wrong, it's the biggest country in Europe
test: In EU5 the Pope rules a big chunk of central Italy. What is left of it today? | Only the tiny Vatican City | All of central Italy still | The whole of Rome and Naples | Nothing at all
test: Why did San Marino survive when other small Italian states did not? | It stayed small, neutral and on its mountain, and was left alone | It had the biggest army in Italy and beat everyone who attacked | It was hidden on an island | It was part of France
test: You stand in a kitchen in Baarle with one foot in Belgium and one in the Netherlands. Is that possible? | Yes, the border runs through some houses | No, borders are never allowed to cross houses | Only in a video game | Only in the year 1337
test: If Britain left Gibraltar tomorrow, which country would most likely take it over? | Spain | Morocco | Argentina | Portugal
test: Why did Britain want Gibraltar? | It guards the narrow strait between the Atlantic and the Mediterranean | It had huge gold and silver mines deep inside the Rock, worked since Roman times | It was the biggest farmland in Europe | It was the birthplace of Queen Victoria
test: my cousin says Kaliningrad's in Poland. nah? | No, it's part of Russia, between Poland and Lithuania | Yes, it is Poland's capital and its biggest port on the Baltic | No, it's in Lithuania | Yes, Poland bought it in 1991
test: A scientist from Australia and one from Argentina both work in Antarctica. Who owns the land under their station? | No one; claims are frozen by the Antarctic Treaty | Australia owns all of it, and Argentina pays rent | Argentina owns all of it | The scientist who arrived first
test: Your friend says Lesotho is an island. What is wrong? | It is on land, completely surrounded by South Africa | Nothing, it is an island in the Indian Ocean near Madagascar | It is in Europe | It is under the sea
test: In 1982 two countries fought a short war over islands in the South Atlantic. Which? | Britain and Argentina | Spain and Portugal | India and Pakistan | China and Taiwan
test: A pub quiz card says Monaco is the smallest country in the world. How do you fix it? | Vatican City is the smallest, and Monaco is second | San Marino is the smallest, Monaco is third | Monaco is right, the Vatican is not a country | Liechtenstein is the smallest, Monaco is second
test: Which place has its own government, army and elections, though most countries do not officially recognise it? | Taiwan | Switzerland | Andorra | Liechtenstein
exam: Identify the sovereign state of least territorial extent in the world. | Vatican City | Monaco | San Marino | Andorra
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an enclave? | A territory of one state wholly encompassed by another state | A state possessing no coastline | An island situated in the open ocean at a great distance from any continent | A city exercising rule over a province
exam: Ascertain the legal status of Antarctica pursuant to the Antarctic Treaty of 1959. | It is owned by no state and is reserved for peace and science | It is the sovereign territory of the United Kingdom | It is a protectorate of Argentina | It is a member state of the United Nations
exam: State the year from which Gibraltar has been held as a British territory. | 1713 | 1492 | 1982 | 1959
